Perfect 👍 — here’s your updated Python script that:

Reads accession numbers from a text file

Fetches the corresponding TaxIDs using Biopython’s Entrez

Removes duplicates (using set() for uniqueness)

Writes a text file containing only unique TaxIDs (no header)

from Bio import Entrez
from time import sleep

# Set your email (required by NCBI)
Entrez.email = "your.email@domain.com"

# Input and output files
input_file = "accessions.txt"
output_file = "taxids.txt"

# Read accession numbers
with open(input_file) as f:
    accessions = [line.strip() for line in f if line.strip()]

taxids = []

for acc in accessions:
    try:
        # Fetch summary information
        handle = Entrez.esummary(db="nucleotide", id=acc)
        record = Entrez.read(handle)
        handle.close()

        # Extract TaxID
        taxid = record[0]["TaxId"]
        taxids.append(taxid)

        print(f"{acc} → TaxID {taxid}")

        # Be polite to NCBI
        sleep(0.4)

    except Exception as e:
        print(f"Error fetching {acc}: {e}")
        sleep(1)

# --- Remove duplicate TaxIDs ---
unique_taxids = sorted(set(taxids))

# --- Write out only unique TaxIDs (no header) ---
with open(output_file, "w") as f:
    for tid in unique_taxids:
        f.write(f"{tid}\n")

print(f"Done! Wrote {len(unique_taxids)} unique TaxIDs to {output_file}.")

✅ Notes

Replace "your.email@domain.com" with your real email — NCBI requires this.

The script uses sorted(set(taxids)) to ensure no duplicates and a clean, ordered output.

It pauses between requests with sleep(0.4) to respect NCBI rate limits (max ~3 requests/sec).

