####Question: does the output of adapterremoval always have .truncated? 
 Expected Output Files

Because you used --barcode-list, this is a demultiplexing run.
AdapterRemoval will:

Create one output file set per barcode.

Use the --basename as a prefix.

Append each barcode ID to the output name.

You will typically get files like:

IMAS-Dino.1.pair1.truncated
IMAS-Dino.1.pair2.truncated
IMAS-Dino.2.pair1.truncated
IMAS-Dino.2.pair2.truncated
...
IMAS-Dino.settings
IMAS-Dino.log


The numeric suffix (e.g. .1, .2, etc.) corresponds to the order of barcodes in your barcodes_ind6ATGGAGA_dinos.txt.

If your barcode list has barcode identifiers (like sample names) instead of numeric indices, the names might instead look like:

IMAS-Dino_SampleA.pair1.truncated
IMAS-Dino_SampleA.pair2.truncated
IMAS-Dino_SampleB.pair1.truncated
IMAS-Dino_SampleB.pair2.truncated

🧠 Key Notes

If you omit --collapse, AdapterRemoval leaves files unmerged, so you’ll have pair1 and pair2 files (perfect for nf-core/eager input).

If you had used --collapse, it would instead create files like .collapsed.truncated, which nf-core/eager treats differently.
####Question: what really IS the difference between demultiplexing and adapterremoval?

Demultiplexing vs Adapter Removal

These two processes both happen early in the sequencing data workflow, but they solve different problems.

🔹 Demultiplexing

Goal: Separate mixed reads from different samples into individual files.

When it happens:
Usually after sequencing, when one sequencing run (lane) contains data from multiple samples that were tagged with short barcode sequences.

Example:
Suppose you sequenced 3 samples (A, B, C) together. Each fragment got a small barcode:

Sample	Barcode	Example read start
A	ATGC	ATGCACGTTAGG...
B	CGTA	CGTATTAGGCTA...
C	TTAA	TTAACTGATCGA...

After sequencing, all reads are in one file (lane1_R1.fastq.gz and lane1_R2.fastq.gz).

Demultiplexing uses those barcode sequences to split the reads into separate files:

sampleA_R1.fastq.gz
sampleA_R2.fastq.gz
sampleB_R1.fastq.gz
sampleB_R2.fastq.gz
sampleC_R1.fastq.gz
sampleC_R2.fastq.gz

🔹 Adapter Removal

Goal: Remove leftover adapter sequences that were used during library prep (to attach DNA fragments to the flow cell).

When it happens:
After demultiplexing, or if you only have one sample per run (so no demultiplexing was needed).

Adapters show up when your read is longer than your actual DNA fragment.
Example before trimming:

Read: ACTGCTAGTCGATCGATCGATCGATCGATCGATCGATCGATCGATCGATCGATCGATCGATCG
                         ↓
Adapter:        AGATCGGAAGAGCACACGTCTGAACTCCAGTCAC


Adapter trimming removes that unwanted tail sequence, so downstream alignment only uses true genomic DNA.

🔹 Where AdapterRemoval fits in

AdapterRemoval can do both tasks:

If you provide --barcode-list, it demultiplexes first.

If you provide --adapter-list, it trims adapters.

You can run one or both at once (which is what your script does).

That’s why it’s so popular for ancient DNA data — you can go from raw Illumina reads → clean, demultiplexed, trimmed reads in one command.

